.TH CHASHMAP_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chashmap_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at a hash map's first entry
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

cmap_iterator *chashmap_begin_iter(chmap chm, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator on the map's first entry. Each step gives the key
and the value of one entry, which you read with the unified accessors
ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3). The value pointer points
at the map's own storage, so writing through it updates the stored value
in place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro picks this function for a chmap and is
the normal way in. Call this function directly only when you already
know the container type and want the error string.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm   Hash map to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer that receives an error string on failure, or NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to a newly allocated iterator on the first entry.
NULL with *err set to NULL when there is nothing to iterate (chm is NULL
or the map holds no entries).
NULL with *err set to an error string when the iterator itself could not
be allocated.
.fi
.SH "ITERATION ORDER"
.nf
The order depends on the map's implementation, which
chmap_create_full(3) describes. With either implementation the order is
not part of the map's contract, so do not depend on it.

Separate chaining keeps a list of every live entry and links each new
entry at its head, so the iterator starts at the newest entry and works
back to the oldest one.

Open addressing has no such list. The iterator walks the slot array from
the first slot to the last, so the order follows where each key hashed
to and not when it was inserted. The hash depends on the mode of the
map, and the keyed mode depends on a secret of the process, so that
order changes when the map switches to the keyed mode and from then on
differs from one process to the next. See chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL first thing on entry. To tell an empty
result from a failure, test *err and not the return value.
A NULL chm iterates as an empty map and reports no error. This contract
is shared on purpose with cvector_begin_iter(3) and cbmap_begin_iter(3),
so you can iterate a lazily created map field that is still NULL
because nothing was put in it yet, without a NULL check of your own.
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
entry and returns NULL. An iteration that stops early must end with
ccol_iter_destroy(3), or you can declare the iterator with
ccol_iter_declare(3) so that leaving the scope destroys it.
The iterator is allocated through the map's own memory management
procedures. For a map whose values need a destructor on each entry,
chmap_destroy_with_dtor(3) is the better way to destroy it, since it
walks the map without allocating anything while an iterator doing the
same job can fail under memory pressure.
An insert into the map or a removal from it invalidates an open
iterator. Changing a value in place through ccol_iter_val_ptr(3) does
not.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(scores, char *, int);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(scores, it);
for (it = chashmap_begin_iter(scores, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%s = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy_with_dtor (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cvector_begin_iter (3)
